<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Four Books and Five Classics</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Four Books, Five Classics, Sìshū, Wǔjīng</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Four Books and Five Classics are authoritative books associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a>, written before 300 BCE and traditionally believed to have been written, edited or commented on by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a> or his disciples. The Five Classics, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/classic-of-poetry">Classic of Poetry</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-documents">Book of Documents</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yijing">I Ching</a> and the Spring and Autumn Annals, became part of the state curriculum under the Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>. The Four Books, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/analects">Analects</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mencius">Mencius</a>, were selected by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-xi">Zhu Xi</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> as an introduction to Confucian thought. In the Ming and Qing dynasties they formed the core of the civil service examinations.</p></div>
